00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:05.970
So back to 1600s Europe,

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:09.180
where Switzerland remained
this one peaceful confederacy

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.283
protected by its citizen militia.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.100
Meanwhile, in the rest of Europe,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.190
greedy kings were using
their huge standing armies

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:18.330
to conquer one another

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:19.650
while Switzerland stood

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:22.473
by training its citizens
in the ways of war.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:28.500
Swiss citizens got so good at fighting

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:31.740
that the Pope hired them
as his personal protectors.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.050
Other empires would
even hire Swiss soldiers

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.060
to fight their own wars.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:39.240
But Switzerland itself
remained almost entirely out

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.400
of these conflicts on the continent.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.590
Eventually, European thinkers start

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:47.790
to notice this peculiar Alpine
Confederacy, Switzerland,

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:50.400
this place that trains
their citizens to fight

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:51.930
and miraculously showed

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.380
that you can have a republic
even in Europe, a sea

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:57.363
of monarchy and kings.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:03.330
I've been reading a lot

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:05.970
of like old European political literature

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.340
and you start to see this phrase pop up

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.325
to describe how Switzerland defends itself

00:14:10.325 --> 00:14:13.323
using a well-regulated militia.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:16.680
I saw this term a lot when I was looking

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.080
through this old literature.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.560
Around 1610, this British
diplomat goes to Switzerland

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.710
and gawks at their well-regulated militia,

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:28.470
how they can overturn their
government at pleasure

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.600
because they all know how to fight.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.430
By the way, back in like the 1600s

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:36.390
well-regulated militia
meant like a well-organized

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.450
or efficiently run
militia, not like regulated

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:40.800
in the way that we think of it today,

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:44.040
but yeah, the idea was if you
arm everyone in your society

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.080
and you train them,
that's actually the key

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.243
to the best defense and
the best preservation

00:14:48.243 --> 00:14:50.883
of freedom for the
people, not just the king.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:54.630
This idea of a
well-regulated militia spread

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.990
across the ocean to a group
of British intellectuals

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.963
who were secretly conspiring
to overthrow their own king.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.381
In 1776, they declared independence

00:15:04.381 --> 00:15:07.350
using their own well-regulated militia

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:11.160
to fight off a tyrannical
king and his standing army.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:14.100
As they fought this war, a
leader from Switzerland wrote

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.200
to Benjamin Franklin suggesting

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.260
that this new rebel experiment
called the United States

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.713
should become Sister
Republics with Switzerland.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:26.130
The Americans somehow won this war

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.563
and got to work designing
their new country.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.030
They sat down in a hot
church looking building

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:34.320
to draft the document

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.080
that would be the foundation
of this experiment.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.925
A system free of kings.

00:15:39.925 --> 00:15:42.090
The founders studied the political systems

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.710
of Greece and Rome.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.960
They studied the British Magna Carta,

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.413
Native American confederacies,
European philosophers.

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.230
But then there's John
Adams, one of the founders

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.420
who was kind of obsessed with
Switzerland at this time.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:00.540
He studied their system and he was amazed

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.220
at how their militia worked,

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.640
how they required everyone to
have a gun and to have ammo

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.890
and they wouldn't let them
get married unless they did.

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.440
He wrote all about this in like a treaties

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:12.420
on different government systems right

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:14.400
before he wrote the Constitution

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:15.720
with his fellow co-founders.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.340
Switzerland was a model.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.205
It was the republic that
had resisted tyranny

00:16:20.205 --> 00:16:24.060
and been able to stay free
in a Europe full of kings.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.126
And when it came time to
write the actual constitution,

00:16:26.126 --> 00:16:28.920
you can see that a militia was enshrined

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.270
in the Constitution itself.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:32.670
In the Constitution, the
role of the militia is

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:34.990
to protect the government,
to protect the country

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:37.743
from invasion or insurrection.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:44.730
And then after the Constitution,
it became time to draft

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.340
that sacred list of the
most fundamental rights

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:50.460
that would ensure that the
American project remained free

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:51.750
and full of liberty.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:55.410
The first of these 10 amendments
would be freedom of speech

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:59.160
and of expression, assembly,
religion, and the press.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:02.400
But right after that, they
write the Second Amendment

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:05.820
and it's about the well-regulated
militia being vital,

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:09.480
being necessary for the
security of a free country.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.880
So to have a militia, the
people must have arms.

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.400
They must have a right to have guns.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.010
So now you have the
Constitution, the Bill of Rights,

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:18.120
and the Second Amendment

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:19.440
and then a few years later,

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:22.620
the well-regulated militia
idea got enshrined in law

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:25.920
when one of the first congresses
passed a law that said

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.900
that every man doesn't just
have the right to have a gun,

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.780
but is required to have a gun and ammo

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:33.870
and must train to use it so
that the president could call

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:37.110
on its armed citizens as
its mechanism for defense.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.930
This whole legal framework
was very clearly like,

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:42.840
yes, you can have guns,
but only if you are ready

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.700
to use them to defend our country

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:46.530
from invasion or insurrection.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:49.590
And sure enough, George Washington
had to actually use this.

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:52.860
There was this rebellion
in Pennsylvania over taxes,

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.447
and George Washington
called up his armed citizens

00:17:55.447 --> 00:17:58.110
to march and clamp down on these rebels

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.460
and it totally worked.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.070
The militia system was
working for this young nation

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.770
to ensure its freedoms
with an armed citizenry,

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:07.290
something Switzerland had
been doing for centuries.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.888
Guns started out as a symbol
of collective security,

00:18:10.888 --> 00:18:14.100
as collective defense,
armed citizens ready

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:15.930
to stand up to defend the country.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:18.645
But needless to say,
things kind of changed

00:18:18.645 --> 00:18:20.700
when it came to guns in America.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:24.630
So how we got from that to this

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:26.880
is the next part of this video.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:28.830
And in my mind, it's the
most interesting version

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.560
of this story because it
shows us how Switzerland

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:32.880
and the United States diverged

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.190
so severely from this militia culture.

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:36.690
Okay, but before we get to that, I want

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.720
to show you more about Switzerland today.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.910
Show you what it looks
like when a country sticks

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.220
to an actual militia system

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.588
as their primary tool for
national defense. Okay.

00:18:46.588 --> 00:18:49.171
